  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Operator's Console",
    "overview": "The site shows the alarm system at work rather than describing it. Its surfaces are what an operator would see: deep navy control panels with a faint measuring grid, hairline-divided panes, detectors listed with status dots, timestamps in tabular figures, a log that ends in a decision. Around those control surfaces sits a cool, near-white ground where the reading happens, with brand blue as the working colour for links, active states and small markers.\n\nThe mood is technical and exact, calm rather than alarmed. Type is Archivo at near-normal width in sentence case, tight-tracked in headings, with small uppercase micro-labels reserved for data keys and pane titles. Depth is quiet: 1px hairlines, soft layered shadows, rounded but not soft corners (8 to 18px). Brand yellow is a signal, never a surface: it marks the one primary action and the one live or decided thing in a view.\n\nImagery is real company photography (detectors, sites, scaffolding). Inside the system panel a photo is treated as a verification still: greyscale, raised contrast, with a thin yellow detection box and dark overlay captions.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Navy control surfaces (hero, flow section, CTA band, footer, mobile callbar) alternating with cool-white and white reading surfaces.",
      "Brand blue as the working accent; yellow only for primary action and live/decision marks.",
      "Hairline grids (1px gaps over the rule colour) to divide data into fields and panes.",
      "Uppercase micro-labels plus tabular figures for anything that is data: IDs, times, phone numbers.",
      "Motion that resolves a state once (event arrives, log lines appear, timeline draws) and then rests."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Signal Rule",
        "body": "Yellow is a mark, not a field. At most one yellow action per view region plus live/decision marks; never a section background, card fill, or heading colour on light ground.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Working Blue Rule",
        "body": "Interactive state on light ground is always brand blue (active, focus, hover affordances). Do not introduce a second accent hue.",
        "section": "colors"
